WEBVTT

NOTE
This file was generated by Descript <www.descript.com>

00:00:00.520 --> 00:00:03.550
Maddy: Knowing how to create and
maintain an effective team is an

00:00:03.550 --> 00:00:05.310
important part of team leadership.

00:00:05.790 --> 00:00:10.220
Phillip: But it’s important to realise that
the evolution of teams and their good running

00:00:10.559 --> 00:00:13.170
isn’t always a clear and linear process.

00:00:13.730 --> 00:00:16.950
It can often be unpredictable
and can even be a little chaotic!

00:00:17.660 --> 00:00:21.730
Using a model is a good way to prepare for
the likely events you’ll encounter, but

00:00:22.110 --> 00:00:26.850
there’s always going to be some small distance
between the content of the model and reality.

00:00:27.550 --> 00:00:32.150
Let’s look at the Tuckman’s team development
model and some of the general principles around

00:00:32.170 --> 00:00:35.099
creating and maintaining an effective team

00:00:36.100 --> 00:00:39.450
Maddy: Tuckman’s model is really
helpful for plotting out the evolution

00:00:39.450 --> 00:00:43.450
that a team goes through, and it
breaks this down into four main steps:

00:00:44.480 --> 00:00:45.030
• Forming

00:00:45.480 --> 00:00:46.120
• Storming

00:00:46.719 --> 00:00:47.249
• Norming

00:00:47.790 --> 00:00:48.390
• Performing

00:00:49.090 --> 00:00:53.949
There is also a fifth step called Mourning
which is also known as adjourning.

00:00:54.629 --> 00:00:56.370
Let’s look at these in more detail.

00:00:57.390 --> 00:01:00.620
Phillip: Forming refers to when
the team starts to come together.

00:01:01.390 --> 00:01:05.919
There’s a lot of uncertainty at times
so strong leadership and clear goals

00:01:05.990 --> 00:01:08.010
are especially important at this point.

00:01:09.270 --> 00:01:11.099
Maddy: Storming is when the work has begun.

00:01:11.559 --> 00:01:15.510
The team now must reckon with reality
and will inevitably experience

00:01:15.510 --> 00:01:17.220
resistant of some kind or another.

00:01:17.940 --> 00:01:22.059
Team members will also be working with one
another, and this will probably produce

00:01:22.090 --> 00:01:24.490
some conflicts and raise some problems too.

00:01:25.590 --> 00:01:28.789
Phillip: Norming is when the team
has started to get its act together.

00:01:29.410 --> 00:01:33.630
Differences within the team are being
reconciled, realistic expectations around

00:01:33.630 --> 00:01:38.210
team goals have been established, and
the real work is finally being done.

00:01:39.110 --> 00:01:42.070
Maddy: Performing is when is the team at its peak.

00:01:42.400 --> 00:01:47.429
It’s working as a well-oiled machine, hitting
its targets, and operating efficiently.

00:01:48.059 --> 00:01:53.220
Phillip: Mourning the fifth step refers to
the point when a team is being wound down.

00:01:53.780 --> 00:01:59.480
Some teams may not go through this step and
continue indefinitely, but ones that reach an end

00:01:59.490 --> 00:02:05.079
point, for whatever reason, will undergo a period
of reflection and probably a little sadness.

00:02:05.600 --> 00:02:09.699
Maddy: Tuckman’s model is useful as
it covers the whole team lifecycle,

00:02:10.100 --> 00:02:13.730
and it gives you the landmarks of team
development and maintenance to look out for.

00:02:14.490 --> 00:02:18.709
By regularly reviewing how your team is
functioning, you can determine which of

00:02:18.730 --> 00:02:23.119
the Tuckman’s stages have been reached,
as well as the next steps you need to take

00:02:23.129 --> 00:02:25.000
to develop your team onto the next level.

00:02:25.580 --> 00:02:31.290
But remember that this isn’t always a linear
process and teams can go “backward” as

00:02:31.290 --> 00:02:36.470
well as “forward”, going down to a previous
level, instead of onwards to the next.

00:02:37.429 --> 00:02:41.600
Phillip: Let’s look at some more general
principles for creating and maintaining a team.

00:02:41.600 --> 00:02:46.190
It is important to make sure that the
team’s purpose is clearly defined.

00:02:46.700 --> 00:02:50.509
This might be a one-off project, or
it could be permanent working group.

00:02:50.830 --> 00:02:55.480
Whatever it is, it should be made clear,
because it will affect your choices when

00:02:55.480 --> 00:02:57.050
it comes to choosing the right people.

00:02:57.799 --> 00:02:59.779
Maddy: So, who are the right people?

00:03:00.470 --> 00:03:03.920
You should try and find a balance of
fresh perspectives and experience.

00:03:04.480 --> 00:03:06.850
Look out for people with
the right skills and talent.

00:03:07.460 --> 00:03:11.729
Also, you should think hard about the
pool you’re drawing from, do some of

00:03:11.730 --> 00:03:15.160
the people work better together or
maybe they should be placed with others?

00:03:15.719 --> 00:03:17.650
These are important factors to consider.

00:03:18.369 --> 00:03:22.280
Phillip: Make sure you schedule regular reviews
and catchups, so that everyone is clear on

00:03:22.280 --> 00:03:26.600
their duties and responsibilities, but not
so regular that they become a nuisance.

00:03:27.240 --> 00:03:30.730
Maddy: And finally, it’s really important,
that you have multiple channels and

00:03:30.740 --> 00:03:33.400
methods for giving and receiving feedback.

00:03:33.940 --> 00:03:38.270
Your team should develop a culture of giving
each other feedback, with some kind of formal

00:03:38.270 --> 00:03:42.859
process around this, especially when it comes
to giving feedback about team leadership.

